From 82607d435816bdc4984556c989044a4853121868 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 13:15:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: the graphics threads, slow presents and the CPU in
 the log

For telling from a session log which part of a player's PC held the game
back (a Ryzen 5600X tester's report had only frame rates):

- thread_cpu: the FIFO translation worker, Smooth Motion's helper and the
  render worker register themselves; DolAuroraFrameTiming carries their CPU
  time (Windows GetThreadTimes, macOS thread_info, Linux the thread's CPU
  clock), so a host can print how busy each was.
- A present that holds the game thread 100 ms or more writes [present-slow]
  with its parts (the wait for the worker's batch, the end of the frame, the
  window's events, the next frame's begin); an event pump of 100 ms or more
  writes [events-slow], SDL's pump or the host's handlers and the last event
  type. The freezes of 0.4 to 1.2 s seen on the test PC were SDL's pump in
  NtUserMessageCall: another program's window answering late when focus
  changed.
- system_info: the CPU model from CPUID (the WMI query failed after the
  Windows host's disc picker had set up COM security, and the log read
  Unknown; RPC_E_TOO_LATE is now accepted by the fallback), and a CPU cores
  line with performance and efficiency cores and threads.
